# The exr provenance gate hashes the original crates.io bytes. Keep Git from
# rewriting LF to CRLF on Windows checkouts before those hashes are verified.
# Upstream also contains intentional whitespace that must remain byte-identical.
/crates/vendor/exr/** -text -whitespace

# `git apply` matches patch context BYTE for byte, and the pristine crates.io sources these
# describe are LF. On a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true Git would hand the script a
# CRLF patch, every context line would miss, and vendor-jxl.ps1 would fail on a clean clone
# for a reason that has nothing to do with the tree. Keep them exactly as committed.
/crates/vendor/jxl-patches/*.patch -text

# Same reasoning as the jxl-patches line below: `git apply -p2` matches context BYTE for
# byte, and vendor-exr.ps1 normalizes the pristine tarball's 4 touched files to LF before
# diffing/applying (see that script's header for why this crate needs that extra step, unlike
# jxl). A Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true would hand it a CRLF patch instead.
/crates/vendor/exr-patches/*.patch -text

# Same reasoning as the exr line above, for the two trees the jxl patches are applied to:
# vendor-jxl.ps1 -Check regenerates them from pristine (LF) sources and diffs the result
# against the committed copy. Without this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true hands
# the diff a CRLF-converted committed tree against an LF-regenerated one, and every line
# reports as drift that has nothing to do with the actual vendored content.
/crates/vendor/jxl-render/** -text
/crates/vendor/jxl-oxide/** -text
